## 23. IMPLEMENTATION WORKFLOW

Follow this sequence.

### Phase 1 — Audit

Inspect the repository, existing assets, package dependencies, and framework.

Identify the supplied navigation component and determine how it should be integrated.

Confirm where the provided logo will be placed.

Do not delete or overwrite useful files without understanding their role.

### Phase 2 — Design system

Establish the color tokens, typography, spacing, layout grid, buttons, labels, borders, and light/dark theme behavior.

Implement the header and fullscreen navigation first.

### Phase 3 — Hero prototype

Build the complete hero composition and a functional first version of the generative artwork.

Validate the typography, spacing, responsive layout, and theme toggle before expanding the page.

### Phase 4 — Motion system

Implement the animation utilities, navigation transitions, reduced-motion handling, and hero scroll choreography.

Keep animation logic organized and reusable.

### Phase 5 — Homepage continuation

Build the selected work, tools, custom software, services, studio, approach, journal, contact, and footer sections.

Ensure that each section has its own visual composition and purpose.

Do not repeat the hero layout for every section.

### Phase 6 — Responsive refinement

Review desktop, tablet, and mobile behavior.

Adjust typography, spacing, image proportions, navigation, and motion independently for each viewport.

### Phase 7 — Quality assurance

Run the project's available checks, including:

- TypeScript validation.
- Linting.
- Production build.
- Responsive checks.
- Navigation testing.
- Light/dark mode testing.
- Keyboard navigation.
- Reduced-motion testing.
- Console and hydration error checks.
- Form behavior testing where applicable.

Fix issues rather than hiding them.

### Phase 8 — Delivery

Provide a concise implementation summary, list of changed files, required environment variables, any remaining placeholders, and exact local-development and deployment instructions.

Do not claim that a feature works unless it has been implemented and verified.

